by MikiBuilder at 2026-07-29T08:04:05Z

Nice to see this landing. I built something in this space outside the framework a few months ago (`mikibuilder/llm-vcr`) and hit two problems that might be worth weighing while the API is still open.

**Signature matching breaks on realistic prompts.** The docs say a replayed call must use the same arguments. That holds for fixed fixtures, but real prompts carry values that change between runs — timestamps, incident IDs, RAG context. Same case semantically, different signature. In practice people end up deleting and re-recording cassettes often, which quietly erodes the value of having them. Normalising volatile tokens before hashing fixed it for me, and making the signature function injectable would leave the door open without committing to a strategy now.

**Cassettes get committed, so they can leak.** Recorded prompts contain whatever the user sent: emails, phone numbers, occasionally an API key pasted by mistake. I ended up making redaction the default rather than opt-in, on the assumption that nobody reads the configuration reference of a dev dependency. One subtle consequence in case it saves someone time: if you redact on write you also have to match on redacted text, otherwise a request carrying a real email never matches its own redacted cassette — and it fails precisely on the requests that contain personal data.

One more thought on dropping token usage: it is the raw material for detecting when a provider silently changes model behaviour. A field going from `int` to `string` breaks a typed DTO in production and no test catches it, because the mocks still hold the value frozen months ago. Not suggesting it belongs in this PR, just that keeping usage data around leaves the option open.

Happy to help if any of this is useful — mine is MIT ([mikibuilder/llm-vcr](https://github.com/MikiBuilder/llm-vcr)) and I am glad to extract any part as a PR here, or leave it as prior art if the approach differs.

