by wachterjohannes at 2026-08-16T18:37:22Z

Thanks, all three are concrete and worth having.

**Signature matching**: agreed, and it was not injectable at all. Pushed a `signature` constructor argument, so volatile parts can be normalized before hashing instead of forcing a re-record.

**Leaking**: this one lands on #2128 rather than here. A provider cassette stores the model, a hash of the input and the result, never the input itself, so there is nothing to redact on the request side and the mismatch trap you describe cannot bite. A recorded answer can of course still repeat back what the prompt contained, which the docs now say. #2128 does commit the request body verbatim, so I will take the point over there.

**Token usage**: agreed on the value, but drift detection cannot live at this layer, since replay returns the already converted result and never runs the converter. That job belongs to #2128, where the raw bytes are kept. Preserving metadata here is still worth doing for tests that want to assert on usage, and it is out of this PR only because metadata holds arbitrary objects and needs its own serialization pass.

Happy to look at anything from `llm-vcr`, the normalization rules especially.

